.TH ROLESWAP 1
.SH NAME
roleswap \- do the receive errors follow the role or the board?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/radio/roleswap.py
.I board1-session
.I board2-session
.RI [ runs-per-arm ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
One station abandons received frames and the other does not.
.B "Two explanations fit and they are not distinguishable from a single arrangement:"
the
.I role
\- the caller mounts and so receives the long 109-byte Styx replies,
while the answerer receives mostly short acknowledgements \- or the
.IR board ,
one part on one spot of one desk with one antenna.
.PP
This swaps
.BR call ,
.B peer
and
.B role
between the two entries in
.IR radiocal (1)'s
.IR /lib/radio ,
flashes both, and runs
.IR twostyx (1)
.I runs-per-arm
times each way.
.B "It costs nothing: no cable, no attenuator, nobody in the room."
.PP
.B ppb ,
.BR rssioffset ,
.B csthr
and
.B rssislope
are deliberately NOT swapped.  They are per-part measurements and
swapping them would move two things at once, which is how this bench
has produced most of its wrong answers.
.SH "WHAT IT FOUND"
.DS
.CW "board1 as caller   stale 1/1 crcbad 1/3"
.CW "board1 as answerer stale 0/0 crcbad 0/0"
.CW "board2 as answerer stale 0/0 crcbad 0/0"
.CW "board2 as caller   stale 2/1 crcbad 0/2"
.DE
.B "Both boards are clean as answerer and dirty as caller."
Four bigreads of four.
.PP
.B "And in the same runs the RSSI stayed with the BOARD"
\- -16 and -17 for board1, -11 for board2, in both arms \- so one
variable followed the role and the other followed the part, measured
together.
.B "That is what makes it a separation and not a coincidence,"
and it is the whole reason to swap rather than to reason.
.SH "COUNTERS BY UID, NOT BY LABEL"
.IR Twostyx 's
session labels mean
.I answerer
and
.IR caller ,
and after a swap those are different boards \-
.B "so reading the near column as \(lqthe ground\(rq silently compares two different parts."
Every station prints its uid at boot and its counters later on lines
carrying the same session prefix, so this keys everything by uid.
.PP
.B "And a \(lqstyx: far\(rq line belongs to the PEER."
.IR Counters ()
prints where a relay direction dies, so on a run that succeeds only the
caller prints its own and the answerer's arrive through
.IR farcounters ()
fetching them over the link.  The first cut excluded them and left the
answerer's columns empty, which was the entire comparison.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/radio
.TP
.B /lib/radio
.BR call ,
.B peer
and
.B role
per board; the rest is left alone
.TP
.B tools/f407/flashverify.sh
the build and the proof the board runs what was flashed
.SH SOURCE
.B tools/radio/roleswap.py
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR ramptrial (1),
.IR radiocal (1),
.IR radio (3),
.B doc/radio-plan.ms
.SH BUGS
Two runs an arm is enough to see a separation this clean and not enough
for a rate; see
.B tools/radio/stalerate.py
for that.
